00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:34.789
Hi, everyone.

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:40.051
I'm William Patton, software developer at
Equalize Digital, and today I'm gonna be

00:02:40.052 --> 00:02:46.868
walking through, um, using WCAG in CI/CD,
catching accessibility bugs the same as

00:02:46.869 --> 00:02:48.630
you would catch any other type of bug.

00:02:49.198 --> 00:02:52.599
So I will be wiring automated
accessibility checks right into

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:59.209
the CI/CD pipeline using GitHub
Actions, axe-core, and Playwright.

00:02:59.878 --> 00:03:03.101
I'll show you how to apply it to
two different systems: a static

00:03:03.101 --> 00:03:06.676
site where we check just the front
end, and a more complicated example

00:03:06.714 --> 00:03:09.906
where we test the admin area and
the output of a WordPress plugin.

00:03:10.643 --> 00:03:15.396
Um, I've used these patterns
day-to-day at work, so I've packaged

00:03:15.409 --> 00:03:19.026
these up into reusable systems and
ready to share them with people.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:23.487
All of the code for the entire talk
will be available on my GitHub, which

00:03:23.504 --> 00:03:25.908
will be linked at the end of this talk.

00:03:28.314 --> 00:03:30.757
So this is how it usually goes.

00:03:31.346 --> 00:03:35.440
Testing for accessibility is
quite often a manual step.

00:03:35.751 --> 00:03:38.426
It depends on someone
actually remembering to do it.

00:03:39.182 --> 00:03:44.723
Um, it quite often feels as if it is
specialist work, something only an

00:03:45.110 --> 00:03:46.976
accessibility expert might be able to do.

00:03:47.144 --> 00:03:49.840
Most developers don't
think they're qualified.

00:03:49.942 --> 00:03:55.569
They don't touch it, and it almost
always happens late, after release,

00:03:55.649 --> 00:03:59.361
when the fix costs much more
than catching it in development.

00:03:59.821 --> 00:04:04.885
Usually spotted by someone visiting
your website, using your plugin, or

00:04:04.885 --> 00:04:06.792
just browsing around the, the internet.

00:04:07.119 --> 00:04:10.011
Not usually spotted by
people on your own team.

00:04:10.745 --> 00:04:15.448
But none of that is because anyone has
ignored accessibility, been negligent.

00:04:15.916 --> 00:04:19.793
It's because the testing's manual
and treat like specialist knowledge

00:04:19.841 --> 00:04:22.005
no one on the team actually has.

00:04:22.683 --> 00:04:26.022
But here's the thing, accessibility
debt is mostly a process

00:04:26.022 --> 00:04:27.768
bug, not a knowledge gap.

00:04:28.328 --> 00:04:32.086
You already know how to write
code, use GitHub, CI/CD.

00:04:32.455 --> 00:04:35.510
What's missing isn't
expertise, it's the wiring.

00:04:38.558 --> 00:04:40.020
So here's the whole pipeline.

00:04:40.279 --> 00:04:43.104
Everything fits inside of
one of these five stages.

00:04:43.441 --> 00:04:47.184
First one is a pull request
triggers the CI to run.

00:04:48.984 --> 00:04:54.982
CI builds and puts the changes where it
needs to be, um, so that it can be tested.

00:04:55.964 --> 00:04:58.719
The scan runs against
that build from the PR.

00:04:59.378 --> 00:05:04.058
Results, results get written into
some form of a report, and in the

00:05:04.058 --> 00:05:07.534
end, branch protection decides whether
that merge is actually allowed.

00:05:08.051 --> 00:05:12.003
So just gonna say upfront, none
of this actually needs CI to run.

00:05:12.059 --> 00:05:16.070
You can actually run all of this on
your local machine as you develop.

00:05:16.472 --> 00:05:20.505
Same configuration, same axe-core,
and get the same report at the end.

00:05:21.222 --> 00:05:25.087
CI is what turns this into the
merge gate, but locally is how you

00:05:25.087 --> 00:05:28.421
would catch a regression before
you ever even have to publish your

00:05:28.421 --> 00:05:29.822
code for anyone else to see it.

00:05:30.527 --> 00:05:35.522
Um, the demos that we'll show later run
against real GitHub, but the same checks

00:05:35.522 --> 00:05:39.219
would work on your laptop, your work
machine, wherever you build your things.

00:05:39.989 --> 00:05:45.155
Um, public repos on GitHub have a pretty
generous amount of runner minutes.

00:05:45.308 --> 00:05:49.782
If you have a paid GitHub account and
you're using private repos, then you're

00:05:49.784 --> 00:05:54.029
actually already paying for the entire
part of this system has any cost at all.

00:05:54.165 --> 00:05:56.498
Everything else is free
open source software.

00:05:58.732 --> 00:06:02.433
So before we get into the demos, here's
the first of the two actions that

00:06:02.433 --> 00:06:08.186
we're gonna run, or the two action
pairs we run in the… in CI/CD.

00:06:08.796 --> 00:06:13.618
So this first one is the scan action,
and underneath it is just Playwright.

00:06:14.522 --> 00:06:18.476
Um, most people might… who've done
any end-to-end testing before will be

00:06:18.476 --> 00:06:20.506
very familiar with how Playwright works.

00:06:20.849 --> 00:06:25.289
Um, but in this configuration, you
just give it a list of URLs, each one

00:06:25.289 --> 00:06:27.551
gets opened in a real headless browser.

00:06:28.053 --> 00:06:31.938
It loads the page, and then once
that page gets rendered, it injects

00:06:31.939 --> 00:06:34.231
axe-core and runs against what it sees.

00:06:34.734 --> 00:06:39.207
It does that for every URL that's in the
list that it gets fed, and it collects

00:06:39.222 --> 00:06:44.474
all of axe-core's results into one package
set that can be processed later on.

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:49.866
Um, so that raw output is what
the next two actions get fed.

00:06:50.128 --> 00:06:55.034
Um, and just to be upfront about this
action, this is the simple option.

00:06:55.433 --> 00:06:58.936
This only scans publicly
available URLs that you feed in.

00:06:59.111 --> 00:07:03.448
It doesn't handle logging in, clicking
run, performing any interactions

00:07:03.498 --> 00:07:08.275
whatsoever, um, which might not be what
you need for your development flow.

00:07:08.593 --> 00:07:12.438
Um, I do have a demo on a WordPress
site that where you would write your

00:07:12.438 --> 00:07:16.787
own interactions, Playwright can run,
but you will need to drive Playwright

00:07:16.787 --> 00:07:18.804
yourself in those complex cases.

00:07:20.948 --> 00:07:23.782
So this is the second part of
the stack, the report actions.

00:07:23.924 --> 00:07:24.806
There's two of them.

00:07:25.197 --> 00:07:28.534
They both do similar things, but
they have different forms of output.

00:07:29.192 --> 00:07:31.668
Um, both of these are
technically optional.

00:07:31.899 --> 00:07:33.317
The gate still works without them.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:34.351
The scan still runs.

00:07:34.352 --> 00:07:36.604
You would still get a
pass or fail at the end.

00:07:36.914 --> 00:07:40.219
But these two actions are what
make the findings actually usable.

00:07:40.811 --> 00:07:46.868
Um, they take the raw scan results
from axe-core, and they do two things.

00:07:46.907 --> 00:07:50.902
The first one is they generate a
markdown report, which gets added

00:07:50.928 --> 00:07:54.823
directly to the GitHub action run,
so it's available in the job summary.

00:07:55.161 --> 00:07:59.748
Anyone who looks on GitHub can check
that summary, see the information.

00:07:59.789 --> 00:08:01.619
They don't have to dig
through the raw results.

00:08:02.139 --> 00:08:05.126
Um, the second one is
an HTML report action.

00:08:05.435 --> 00:08:09.955
It generates an HTML report that
is self-contained, shareable.

00:08:10.269 --> 00:08:13.813
You can give this to someone who
might not be on GitHub day-to-day,

00:08:13.969 --> 00:08:19.705
might not be using, um, your CI or
your CD day-to-day, like a designer,

00:08:19.926 --> 00:08:23.277
project manager, or whoever else
needs to see what the results are.

00:08:23.878 --> 00:08:26.301
Um, you can skip adding these actions.

00:08:26.356 --> 00:08:29.342
You will still get the pass and the
fail, but the difference between

00:08:29.344 --> 00:08:33.259
that and this is you just know the
check failed, but with these reports,

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:35.098
you can actually show people why.

00:08:37.745 --> 00:08:43.512
So we do have two
different sites, um, here.

00:08:43.583 --> 00:08:45.891
So we have a simple one,
which is Demo Bakery.

00:08:46.037 --> 00:08:48.692
It's a small static
site, mostly just HTML.

00:08:48.724 --> 00:08:50.982
There's no complex build site step.

00:08:51.136 --> 00:08:56.132
It's live and runs on GitHub Pages,
and this one is fast enough we can

00:08:56.168 --> 00:09:01.743
actually perform a live run, watch an
accessibility issue, block the merge, and

00:09:01.743 --> 00:09:06.363
see when we fix it, the deploy actually
getting changed and becoming live.

00:09:07.004 --> 00:09:12.038
Um, we also have another site that
is the test bed for a notice plugin.

00:09:12.844 --> 00:09:18.460
Um, most people who are building
plugins are gonna have, um, a

00:09:18.460 --> 00:09:22.572
staging site somewhere, and they are
only gonna wanna test their actual

00:09:22.592 --> 00:09:24.615
plugin, not the entire website.

00:09:24.618 --> 00:09:28.070
So this will be an example of how
you can scope that to just what your

00:09:28.088 --> 00:09:33.629
plugin touches within the context of
a full website and without getting

00:09:33.634 --> 00:09:38.041
blocked by any of the actual theme
issues or other website content issues.

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:44.119
Um, uh, before we break anything,
um, I do wanna point out that in the

00:09:44.119 --> 00:09:46.221
report, we have a breakdown of personas.

00:09:46.413 --> 00:09:50.502
These personas come from the
GOV.UK, the UK government digital

00:09:50.904 --> 00:09:54.591
services, and they're used across
all UK government services.

00:09:55.152 --> 00:10:00.263
Um, they're not just simulations of
people, they're a planning tool for

00:10:00.268 --> 00:10:04.556
thinking about who findings actually
affect in the accessibility world.

00:10:04.908 --> 00:10:09.145
So I've plotted them against the
core rules so that we can measure

00:10:09.146 --> 00:10:12.879
how much of each person's needs
automation can actually detect.

00:10:13.434 --> 00:10:17.015
So we have high coverage
for Ashley and Ron.

00:10:17.107 --> 00:10:20.929
Ashley r- uses a screen reader
and requires good page semantics.

00:10:21.462 --> 00:10:24.597
Um, Ron needs large text and simple form.

00:10:25.747 --> 00:10:29.188
Um, we have good coverage
for Claudia and Christopher.

00:10:29.439 --> 00:10:33.681
Claudia uses a magnifier and
relies on webpage having sufficient

00:10:33.681 --> 00:10:36.534
contrast so that she can identify
the breaks between things.

00:10:37.095 --> 00:10:41.938
Christopher navigates primarily with
keyboard, but also with speech input.

00:10:43.073 --> 00:10:46.683
Um, there is only moderate
coverage for Simone and Saleem.

00:10:47.214 --> 00:10:51.854
Simone relies on having readable
structure on the page so that she

00:10:51.854 --> 00:10:53.693
can navigate around and find herself.

00:10:54.114 --> 00:10:59.170
Um, Saleem needs, Saleem needs
captions and ways to contact

00:10:59.176 --> 00:11:00.943
people that are not audio-based.

00:11:01.846 --> 00:11:06.435
And we only have low coverage
with automation for Pawel, who

00:11:06.492 --> 00:11:08.291
requires low cognitive load.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:11.922
He needs to be able to understand
what's on the page clearly

00:11:12.121 --> 00:11:13.813
without too many distractions.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:21.390
And the handoff here is if you
have one label violation on a page,

00:11:21.502 --> 00:11:25.086
it actually becomes impact screen
reader and speech input users,

00:11:25.384 --> 00:11:27.263
affects Ashley and Christopher.

00:11:27.519 --> 00:11:31.562
So converts the rule ID to a
person by name, and that helps an

00:11:31.563 --> 00:11:34.473
awful lot of people to understand
why something is important.

00:11:36.475 --> 00:11:43.437
So in CI, we have workflows, and this
is the, the file that's actually gonna

00:11:43.528 --> 00:11:47.305
do the bulk of the work for us, where
we'll configure all of our setup.

00:11:47.877 --> 00:11:54.551
So we have, um, four mechanics
specifically on this one, um, or four

00:11:54.555 --> 00:11:56.237
mechanics that matter in the workflow.

00:11:56.251 --> 00:12:00.039
Workflows typically have a lot of
other steps like checkout, set up

00:12:00.039 --> 00:12:04.733
node, composer, run and install,
cache layers, but none of them

00:12:04.733 --> 00:12:06.486
matter for this purpose here.

00:12:06.582 --> 00:12:10.594
All we need is to care about is
that we can build the PR target,

00:12:11.454 --> 00:12:16.691
run axe against that target, report
and enforce those thresholds that

00:12:16.691 --> 00:12:22.627
we have set, and then fail when the
checks actually discover problems.

00:12:23.355 --> 00:12:28.964
So the, the fail at the end is the gate
that decides whether something is gonna

00:12:28.973 --> 00:12:30.970
be mergeable and deployable or not.

00:12:31.770 --> 00:12:33.189
And if we have a quick look.

00:12:33.284 --> 00:12:37.907
So the whole concept here
is fail last, not fast.

00:12:38.172 --> 00:12:41.975
So in GitHub Actions, typically
when a failure is encountered,

00:12:42.057 --> 00:12:43.642
the action run stops.

00:12:44.249 --> 00:12:47.054
But we need to bypass that
default behavior here.

00:12:47.251 --> 00:12:54.009
So the scan step runs with continue on
error set to true, so that if this fails,

00:12:54.425 --> 00:12:56.860
it will still move on to the report step.

00:12:57.516 --> 00:13:00.756
Um, the reporting, aggregating, and
uploading for the artifacts would

00:13:00.756 --> 00:13:05.062
never happen if any of the prior
steps failed in default configuration.

00:13:05.690 --> 00:13:10.362
So that the, the scan
action will always run.

00:13:10.548 --> 00:13:16.165
The report actions will also always run,
and at the end, based on the initial

00:13:16.167 --> 00:13:18.498
scan, is when the fail is determined.

00:13:19.309 --> 00:13:24.760
Um, there… This, this workflow here
shows the simple one with the axe

00:13:24.772 --> 00:13:28.656
scan action, but it looks much the
same in a more complex situation.

00:13:29.095 --> 00:13:31.570
So if we look at this one, the
first step will actually be

00:13:31.668 --> 00:13:33.419
calling Playwright specifically.

00:13:34.264 --> 00:13:38.094
Uh, when you call that, you will need to
configure it yourself, drive it yourself,

00:13:38.152 --> 00:13:39.960
and set up all of the interactions.

00:13:42.633 --> 00:13:44.022
So what should we scan?

00:13:44.934 --> 00:13:49.599
So when I'm doing these scans, I break
things into three separate groups.

00:13:49.731 --> 00:13:53.360
First off, the, the site itself,
which are generally the public pages,

00:13:53.901 --> 00:13:57.258
um, potentially different states for
those pages and the more advanced

00:13:57.259 --> 00:14:02.072
use cases, like different appearances
when someone's logged in, if there's

00:14:02.072 --> 00:14:06.615
modals, how to scan it when the module
is open, or any other interaction

00:14:06.623 --> 00:14:08.006
that user might be able to reach.

00:14:08.957 --> 00:14:12.574
Um, there's also core pages, which,
you know, stock WordPress screens.

00:14:12.639 --> 00:14:16.446
They're normally not gonna be something
you're gonna have to test against.

00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:19.382
You would only need to test against
them if you were building something

00:14:19.399 --> 00:14:23.415
on top of one of those pages or
specifically on one of those pages.

00:14:24.128 --> 00:14:28.518
And then there's the admin step or the
admin group, which is, you know, your

00:14:28.518 --> 00:14:33.585
authenticated plugin surfaces, your
settings pages, setup wizard, custom

00:14:33.585 --> 00:14:35.932
dashboard widgets, anything that you…

00:14:36.017 --> 00:14:40.966
a logged in user has to, uh, make use of
while they're logged into the admin area.

00:14:41.413 --> 00:14:46.421
And this one requires a logged in session,
which is where most tooling stops.

00:14:46.621 --> 00:14:50.051
They typically don't handle the
logins and tackle any of this.

00:14:50.953 --> 00:14:54.519
So let's break some things.

00:14:55.752 --> 00:15:01.563
Um, so first we'll start with the,
the simpler site, and let me just

00:15:02.168 --> 00:15:10.034
switch to my browser, and I will
show you this demo bakery site.

00:15:10.297 --> 00:15:12.979
So this is what you might expect
for a typical marketing site.

00:15:12.979 --> 00:15:20.979
It has a hero, a few buttons, some product
images, general marketing speak, and some

00:15:21.018 --> 00:15:23.670
content here at the end in the footer.

00:15:23.738 --> 00:15:27.622
So this, you know, is a,
is a fairly simple site.

00:15:28.113 --> 00:15:32.388
There's nothing here that
fails on an axe-core scan.

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:37.347
But if we were to take a look at one
of these pull requests, and if someone

00:15:37.347 --> 00:15:42.374
was to add a holiday banner onto the
homepage, if we have a quick look

00:15:42.425 --> 00:15:44.206
at what that code change looks like.

00:15:44.354 --> 00:15:48.962
So there is some CSS that's being added
for the promo banner, the promos text.

00:15:49.086 --> 00:15:53.828
It sets a color, some alignment,
and adds a section to the homepage.

00:15:54.338 --> 00:15:59.006
So the promo text section just says,
"Holiday pre-orders are open. Collect

00:15:59.013 --> 00:16:03.325
from twentieth of December." So from
this code diff, nothing stands out

00:16:03.372 --> 00:16:09.039
as being an accessibility problem
whatsoever to me, and presumably

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:10.453
to most people, none of this does.

00:16:10.925 --> 00:16:16.146
But realistically, this color here
doesn't pass on the background.

00:16:16.563 --> 00:16:20.171
Now, it's almost… It is impossible
to tell from this single diff.

00:16:20.177 --> 00:16:21.527
You don't know what the color is.

00:16:21.789 --> 00:16:23.467
You don't know what the
contrast ratios are.

00:16:23.967 --> 00:16:27.689
So if we take a quick look in my IDE.

00:16:31.598 --> 00:16:31.716
Yeah.

00:16:32.052 --> 00:16:36.707
So here, here in my IDE, we have
the holiday pre-orders are open

00:16:36.708 --> 00:16:40.233
text, the same text we saw on
GitHub where the, the diff was.

00:16:40.591 --> 00:16:43.769
And if we take a look at the
style rules, they're the same.

00:16:44.367 --> 00:16:48.518
